[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       Manage the set of repositories ("remotes") whose branches you track.

[1mOPTIONS[0m
       [1m-v[22m, [1m--verbose[0m
           Be a little more verbose and show remote url after name. For promisor remotes, also show which filters
           ([1mblob:none [22metc.) are configured. NOTE: This must be placed between [1mremote [22mand subcommand.

[1mCOMMANDS[0m
       With no arguments, show a list of existing remotes. Several subcommands are available to perform operations on
       the remotes.

       [1madd[0m
           Add a remote named [4m<name>[24m for the repository at [4m<URL>[24m. The command [1mgit fetch [4m[22m<name>[24m can then be used to
           create and update remote-tracking branches [4m<name>[24m[1m/[4m[22m<branch>[24m.

           With [1m-f [22moption, [1mgit fetch [4m[22m<name>[24m is run immediately after the remote information is set up.

           With [1m--tags [22moption, [1mgit fetch [4m[22m<name>[24m imports every tag from the remote repository.

           With [1m--no-tags [22moption, [1mgit fetch [4m[22m<name>[24m does not import tags from the remote repository.

           By default, only tags on fetched branches are imported (see [1mgit-fetch[22m(1)).

           With [1m-t [4m[22m<branch>[24m option, instead of the default glob refspec for the remote to track all branches under the
           [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/ [22mnamespace, a refspec to track only [4m<branch>[24m is created. You can give more than one [1m-t[0m
           [4m<branch>[24m to track multiple branches without grabbing all branches.

           With [1m-m [4m[22m<master>[24m option, a symbolic-ref [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/HEAD [22mis set up to point at remote’s [4m<master>[0m
           branch. See also the set-head command.

           When a fetch mirror is created with [1m--mirror=fetch[22m, the refs will not be stored in the [1mrefs/remotes/[0m
           namespace, but rather everything in [1mrefs/ [22mon the remote will be directly mirrored into [1mrefs/ [22min the local
           repository. This option only makes sense in bare repositories, because a fetch would overwrite any local
           commits.

           When a push mirror is created with [1m--mirror=push[22m, then [1mgit push [22mwill always behave as if [1m--mirror [22mwas
           passed.

       [1mrename[0m
           Rename the remote named [4m<old>[24m to [4m<new>[24m. All remote-tracking branches and configuration settings for the
           remote are updated.

           In case [4m<old>[24m and [4m<new>[24m are the same, and [4m<old>[24m is a file under [1m$GIT_DIR/remotes [22mor [1m$GIT_DIR/branches[22m, the
           remote is converted to the configuration file format.

       [1mremove[22m, [1mrm[0m
           Remove the remote named [4m<name>[24m. All remote-tracking branches and configuration settings for the remote are
           removed.

       [1mset-head[0m
           Set or delete the default branch (i.e. the target of the symbolic-ref [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/HEAD[22m) for the
           named remote. Having a default branch for a remote is not required, but allows the name of the remote to be
           specified in lieu of a specific branch. For example, if the default branch for [1morigin [22mis set to [1mmaster[22m, then
           [1morigin [22mmay be specified wherever you would normally specify [1morigin/master[22m.

           With [1m-d [22mor [1m--delete[22m, the symbolic ref [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/HEAD [22mis deleted.

           With [1m-a [22mor [1m--auto[22m, the remote is queried to determine its [1mHEAD[22m, then the symbolic-ref
           [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/HEAD [22mis set to the same branch. e.g., if the remote [1mHEAD [22mis pointed at [1mnext[22m, [1mgit remote[0m
           [1mset-head origin -a [22mwill set the symbolic-ref [1mrefs/remotes/origin/HEAD [22mto [1mrefs/remotes/origin/next[22m. This will
           only work if [1mrefs/remotes/origin/next [22malready exists; if not it must be fetched first.

           Use [4m<branch>[24m to set the symbolic-ref [1mrefs/remotes/[4m[22m<name>[24m[1m/HEAD [22mexplicitly. e.g., [1mgit remote set-head origin[0m
